{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>Do not go far to walk.</b> Meaning, a distance of three days. For there is no reason to what Moses said — namely, ״הֵן נִזְבַּח״ etc. ״וְלֹא יִסְקְלוּנוּ״ — if so, why would they need to go a distance of three days? Removing a parsa or slightly more would suffice. And the reason Moses was satisfied with this is: since our entire intent is only to leave the city, what difference does it make to me whether he stipulates one day or two? For if an evil pursuit pursues him, his decree stands against him regardless. Therefore Moses did not wish to scrutinize the condition of time and said, ״רַק אַל יוֹסֵף פַּרְעֹה הָתֵל לְבִלְתִּי שַׁלַּח״ — in whatever manner it may be. And he was precise in saying ״רַק״ — a term of diminution — to say, even if the mission comes about with a condition of less than three days."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:25:54.281831537Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}